.SS "int32_t schultz_layout_is_unbounded (float available)"

.PP
Reports whether an available size means 'no limit'\&. Any negative available size is unbounded\&. This is not an edge case: a context menu wants to be as wide as its widest item with no cap from a parent, a vertical scroll view gives its content unbounded height, and most mobile screens scroll\&. \fBEvery pane must have a defined answer for it\fP, and each built in pane documents its own\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIavailable\fP An available width or height, in pixels\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 when the size is unbounded, 0 when it is a real limit\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_layout_params_default (\fBschultz_layout_params\fP * out_params)"

.PP
Fills in the defaults for per child layout properties\&. Grow never, stretch across the cross axis, grid cell 0,0 spanning one track, the centre slot, and the origin\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIout_params\fP Receives the defaults\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when out_params is NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_layout_measure (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float avail_w, float avail_h, \fBschultz_size\fP * out_size)"

.PP
Measures a node, honouring its pane and its size hints\&. Dispatches to the node's pane when it has one, then clamps the result against the node's minimum and maximum\&. A node with no pane reports its preferred size hints, or zero where they are not set\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to measure\&. 
.br
\fIavail_w\fP Available width, or negative for unbounded\&. 
.br
\fIavail_h\fP Available height, or negative for unbounded\&. 
.br
\fIout_size\fP Receives the measured size\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a NULL pointer, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not live\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_layout_arrange (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_rect\fP rect)"

.PP
Assigns a node its final rectangle and lays out its subtree\&. Sets the node's bounds, which marks the affected regions dirty, then hands over to its pane to place the children\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to place\&. 
.br
\fIrect\fP The final rectangle, in the parent's coordinate space\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a NULL tree, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not live\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_layout_run (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree)"

.PP
Lays out whatever in the tree needs it, and nothing that does not\&. A host running a frame loop calls this once a frame, after resolving styles and before painting\&. It walks down from the root looking for nodes marked as needing layout, arranges each at the bounds it already has, and stops there: arranging a node lays out everything beneath it, so a change deep in the tree costs the subtree it happened in rather than the window\&.
.PP
A node with no pane places nothing, so the walk carries on past it into whatever is below\&. That is what makes a page of absolutely positioned children work: it has no pane, and the panes inside it are found and run\&.
.PP
Nothing is left marked afterwards\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to lay out\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
How many nodes were arranged, which is zero when nothing had changed and the whole call cost one flag test\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_set_anchored (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_rect\fP anchor, uint32_t placement, float gap, int32_t centred, int32_t flips)"

.PP
Keeps a node beside something, and inside the window\&. What a menu and a popover want: sit under the button that opened me, and if that would hang me off an edge, nudge me back in\&. Layout does it every pass rather than once when the node is shown, so the node stays where it belongs when the window changes size\&. On a phone that matters, because a rotation is a resize and it does not dismiss anything\&.
.PP
The node keeps its own size, measured from its contents\&. Only where it sits is decided here\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to place\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIanchor\fP What to sit beside, in the same space as the node's own bounds\&. 
.br
\fIplacement\fP One of SCHULTZ_PLACE_*\&. 
.br
\fIgap\fP Clearance between the node and its anchor, in the placement's own direction\&. Zero sits flush against it\&. 
.br
\fIcentred\fP Nonzero centres the node on the anchor across the axis the placement did not decide: above and below centre it across the anchor's width, left and right across its height\&. Zero lines their leading edges up, which is what a menu under a button wants\&. 
.br
\fIflips\fP Nonzero sends the node to the opposite side when the side asked for does not fit and the other one does, rather than nudging it back inside\&. A tooltip and a popover want this: nudged, they come to rest on top of the thing they are explaining\&. A menu does not, because a submenu that jumps above the row that opened it is disorienting\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_node_anchor_placement (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Which side an anchored node actually ended up on\&. The placement it was given, unless it was allowed to flip and did, in which case the opposite one\&. What a node needs to know to draw anything that points back at its anchor\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields SCHULTZ_PLACE_BELOW\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
One of SCHULTZ_PLACE_*\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_clear_anchored (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

