.SS "int32_t schultz_node_set_places_itself (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t places_itself)"

.PP
Says that something other than a pane decides where a node goes\&. A pane places its children\&. A few nodes are placed by something else and have to be left out of that, or the pane above them drags them into the row or column with everything else and they stop being where they belong\&. A toast is the case in the toolkit: it sits against the edge of the window and stacks with the other toasts, wherever it happens to hang in the tree\&.
.PP
Set this on any node an application places itself\&. Without it, such a node only works while no pane sits above it, which is a rule nothing enforces and nobody can guess: a host that gives its root a pane finds its own hand placed nodes quietly swept into the flow\&.
.PP
An overlay does not need this\&. A dialog, a menu and a popover are already left alone, because the overlay list says the same thing about them\&.
.PP
A node like this is still measured when something asks it for a size, and it is drawn and hit tested as usual\&. Only placing it is somebody else's job\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to mark\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIplaces_itself\fP Nonzero to keep panes off it, zero to let a pane place it again, which is how a node starts\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_places_itself (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether a node is placed by something other than a pane\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 when no pane places it, 0 otherwise\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * schultz_pane_grid (void)"

.PP
Rows and columns, with cells that align across the whole grid\&. This is the pane the composable model needs\&. Nested rows do not align their columns with each other, and no amount of nesting fixes that, so anything genuinely tabular needs real tracks\&.
.PP
Track sizes come from schultz_node_set_grid_tracks\&. A content track is as large as the largest cell in it, a fixed track is exactly its value, and a weighted track shares whatever is left over in proportion to its weight\&. A child spanning several tracks that does not fit grows them evenly\&.
.PP
\fBUnbounded\fP available space means weighted tracks have nothing to share, so they collapse to their content size and the grid reports its natural size\&. Rows and columns beyond SCHULTZ_GRID_MAX_TRACKS are ignored\&.
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The GridPane vtable\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * schultz_pane_vbox (void)"

.PP
A column: children stacked top to bottom\&. Measure sums the children's heights plus the gaps and takes the widest child\&. \fBUnbounded height\fP is passed straight to the children, so a column inside a scroll view reports its full natural height\&. \fBUnbounded width\fP is likewise passed down, and the column reports its widest child\&.
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The VBox vtable\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * schultz_pane_hbox (void)"

.PP
A row: children placed left to right\&. The mirror of VBox\&. Measure sums widths plus gaps and takes the tallest child\&. \fBUnbounded width\fP is passed to the children and the row reports the sum of their natural widths\&.
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The HBox vtable\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * schultz_pane_stack (void)"

.PP
Children share one rectangle, aligned within it\&. Measure returns the largest child size on each axis\&. \fBUnbounded\fP on either axis is passed to the children, and the result is the maximum of what they report\&. Overlays, badges and centred content all fall out of this\&.
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The StackPane vtable\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * schultz_pane_absolute (void)"

.PP
Children at explicit coordinates\&. Each child is placed at the x and y in its layout params, at its preferred size\&. Measure returns the union of the child extents, so an absolute pane still reports a sensible size to whatever contains it\&. \fBUnbounded\fP is passed to the children\&. Children may overflow the pane's bounds; clipping is a separate concern, not a layout constraint\&.
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The absolute Pane vtable\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * schultz_pane_flow (void)"

.PP
Children run across in rows, starting a new row when one is full\&. The pane a tag list, a wrapping toolbar or a gallery of thumbnails wants\&. Its height depends on the width it is offered, which is the case the measure signature exists for and the same shape as wrapping text: narrower means more rows and a taller pane\&.
.PP
A child is as wide as it asks to be and is aligned within its row's height by its own \fCalign\fP, so a row of mixed heights lines up rather than every child stretching\&. The gap sits between children on a row and between the rows themselves\&.
.PP
\fBUnbounded\fP width means nothing ever has to wrap, so everything lands on one row and the pane reports the width that row needs\&. That is what a flow pane inside a horizontal scroll view or a context menu asks for\&.
.PP
A child wider than the pane is put on a row of its own and allowed to overflow, in the same way as a word too long for a line: there is nowhere narrower to put it\&.
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The FlowPane vtable\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * schultz_pane_border (void)"

.PP
Five slots: top, bottom, left, right and centre\&. Top and bottom span the full width at their preferred heights, left and right take their preferred widths from what remains, and the centre takes the rest\&. \fBUnbounded\fP on an axis makes the edge children report their natural size and the centre contribute its own\&.
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The BorderPane vtable\&. 
.RE
.PP

